# Daily planning & dispatch protocol

Tool-agnostic procedure for two linked activities:

- **Plan** — gather items, triage each into executor-typed tasks, propose the **agent queue** and the **human's ordered task list** for the day (budgeted), iterate to approval, and record the plan.
- **Dispatch** — run the agent queue; as agents finish, inject the human review/deploy tasks they generate into the human list at the right priority.

All *configuration* (sources, triage pipelines, priority, estimator, capacity, store) comes from `backlog-sources.mdc` and its overrides — read it first and honor precedence (inline prompt > project override > central default). Planning and dispatch are separate, ephemeral, project-less sessions with no shared local state; they communicate **only** through the configured **plan store**.

Key model (see `backlog-sources.mdc`): one **item** triages into one or more **tasks**, each with an `executor` (`agent` | `agent-assist` | `human`) and `depends_on`. The scarce resource is the **human day**, so agent work is only worth scheduling if the human can absorb the review/deploy it induces.

---

## Phase A — Plan

### A1. Load config
Resolve effective config (+ overrides + inline instructions). Echo the key choices actually used (sources, human_budget, agent_concurrency, store).

### A2. Gather items
Fetch open items from **every** configured source and merge into the normalized item shape. Cover **all** GitHub repos (cross-project) and the `manual` personal source. De-duplicate by `url`. If a source is unreachable, report it and continue.

### A3. Score priority (urgency × impact)
Score each item from **structured** signals, then apply the **raise-only** text scan (quote the phrase; never lower a labeled priority; honor negation). One-line rationale per item.

### A4. Triage items into tasks
For each item, apply the `triage.pipeline` for its `kind` and refine to the item, producing executor-typed tasks with `depends_on`. Tasks **inherit** the item's score (+ `review_boost` for review/deploy). Keep it coarse (`max_tasks_per_item`); downstream tasks (exact review/QA) are templates that **materialize as upstream work completes**.

### A5. Estimate human effort per task
Run the `estimator` for each **human** / **agent-assist** task (by executor). `agent` tasks cost no human budget. Show itemized estimates as conservative guesses with assumptions.

### A6. Build the two queues within budget
- **Agent queue:** the `agent`/`agent-assist` tasks that are ready or will be, bounded by `agent_concurrency`.
- **Human list:** ordered by priority, bounded by `human_budget` and `max_human_tasks`.
- **Reserve for induced load:** when `reserve_for_induced_reviews`, a code item only enters the agent queue if its induced `human_review` + `qa_deploy` estimate also fits `human_budget` alongside the day's chores. Dispatching agent work is **not free** for the human. Items that don't fit are deferred with a short reason.

### A7. Present the plan
Show: the **agent queue** (what will be worked, with the human load each induces), then the **human ordered task list** in two bands — **actionable now** (chores + independent human work) and **expected later today** (reviews/deploys pending agent completion, in anticipated priority position). Include the running human total vs `human_budget` and what was deferred and why.

### A8. Iterate
Treat further input as prioritization/scoping/config overrides (e.g. "must ship X", "drop repo B", "assume 6h", "that's human-only"). Re-triage/re-budget and re-present until the user **explicitly approves**.

### A9. Record the plan
On approval:
- Persist any new **personal items** typed in the prompt as issues in the private `home_repo` (labeled `task`), de-duping against existing open issues — unless flagged "today only".
- Write per-item **tasks** to `task_storage` (sub-issues/checklist on each item).
- Render the **human ordered task list** into the plan store (default: the `Day plan {date}` issue). This projection — not the agent's memory — is the source of truth handed to dispatch. Report where everything was written.
- **End the response with the human's ordered task list** (what to do now), so the user leaves with a clear list while agents work.

---

## Phase B — Dispatch

### B1. Load the plan
Read today's plan/tasks from the store (do not re-plan). If none exists, stop and tell the user to run planning first.

### B2. Run the agent queue (bounded)
Start workers only for `agent`/`agent-assist` tasks whose `depends_on` are satisfied, up to `agent_concurrency`. **Skip `human` tasks** — they belong to the human list, never dispatched to a coding worker. Each code worker follows `feature-workflow.mdc` (git prep → requirements → docs → tests → implement → bounded verify → **auto_review via multi-critic when green**) and **stops at the human_review handoff without committing** (commit stays with `commit-push` unless explicitly authorized). `agent-assist` workers produce a draft for human approval.

### B3. Inject induced human tasks & re-render
When a worker finishes (or blocks), update its task status at the source of truth (`task_storage`), then **materialize the induced human task** — e.g. `human_review` for completed code (with branch/diff link), or `qa_deploy` after review — and **re-render the human ordered task list** in the plan store, inserting the new task at its inherited-priority (+`review_boost`) position. A blocked agent task converts into a human task describing the blocker.

### B4. Refresh (pull)
The human list is a **projection**; there is no long-lived daemon. Re-render it on each planning/dispatch invocation (and whenever the user asks to refresh) from current task statuses, keeping the **actionable now** / **expected later** split.

### B5. Summarize
Report per task: what was done, where the work lives (branch/change), the induced human review/deploy now on the list, and anything blocked. Do not claim a task is deployed — QA/deploy stays a human step within `human_budget`.

---

## Guardrails
- Cross-project & private by default: never assume a single project is in context; the private plan store is the durable hand-off.
- The human day is the budget: never fill the agent queue without reserving for the reviews/deploys it induces.
- Explainable: always show why an item ranked where it did and why it split into those tasks.
- Estimates are conservative guesses, not commitments.
- Never dispatch `human` tasks to an agent; surface them on the human list instead.
